Setting: Grothendieck is describing the boundary ('structure at infinity') of M_{g,ν} combinatorially through ν-graphs G = (S, Ã, σ: Ã→S, ∂, I→S, g: S→N). Here S is the set of vertices (irreducible components), Ã the half-edges, σ the origin map, ∂ the fixed-point-free involution whose orbits are the edges A (nodes), I→S the marked points, and g the genus labelling. A 'special G-pinned curve' (courbe spéciale G-épinglée) is a stable curve over a base, specialising to a curve with dual graph G, with the components, nodes and markings labelled by G.

Previous page: from a subset A' ⊂ A(G) of edges and a subset I' ⊂ I ⊔ J̃ (where J̃ = Ã \ Ã'), one obtains a new special curve with graph G' = (S, A', I'). Geometrically, the nodes outside A' are normalised (or the curve is localised near them). Their preimages become marked points, and one keeps only those in I'. The map I' → S is induced by I → S and by σ on Ã.

This page generalises in one step. Instead of keeping all vertices, one takes an arbitrary, not necessarily full ('partiel') subgraph G' ⊂ G. G' is given by:
- a subset S' ⊂ S of vertices;
- a subset Ã' ⊂ Ã, presumably stable under ∂, with σ(Ã') ⊂ S';
- a subset I' ⊂ I ⊔ J̃ (J̃ = Ã \ Ã') whose image under the induced map I ⊔ J̃ → S lies in S'.

Geometrically this amounts to replacing X' by an open neighbourhood of the components indexed by S' that contains A' and I'. The resulting curve is the union of those components, with the nodes not in A' cut open and the chosen subset of marked points kept.

The key special case is the 'point subgraph' G' = {α}, α ∈ S, so that J̃ = Ã and I' = I_α ⊔ Ã_α, the fibre of I ⊔ Ã → S over α. This yields the normalised component C_α, marked by its original markings plus the preimages of the nodes on it. It is a curve of type (g(α), n(α)), so one gets the projection of a boundary stratum onto the factor M_{g(α), n(α)}. This is the standard identification M̄_G ≅ ∏_v M̄_{g(v), n(v)} (up to automorphisms), seen vertex by vertex.

The last line announces the next page's formalisation: a notion of 'morphisme (homomorphisme) de simplification' of ν-graphs. Such a morphism consists of:
- f_G: G' → G, injective on vertices and on edges;
- f_I: I' ↪ I ⊔ Ã with image in I ⊔ J̃, compatible with the maps to S;
- preservation of genus;
- 'strict' when S' = S and I' = I ⊔ J̃.
These morphisms compose and form a category acting contravariantly on the moduli of special pinned curves. This is part of building the stratified boundary combinatorics needed for the Teichmüller tower and fundamental groupoids at infinity (the program of the Esquisse).
